---

<ChapterLayout
  title="4.0 — Planetary Age · AST 100"
  description="From roughly nine to eleven billion years after the Big Bang — the era when the stardust forged in Chapter 3 gathers into worlds. Four lessons and seven milestones: the architecture of our solar system, the families of planets, the hundred-million-year construction project that built them, and the telescopes that found six thousand more around other stars."
  activeId="4.0"
  chapter={4}
  current="/chapter/4/4.0"
>
  <Hero
    tease="The Stellar Age forged the ingredients; the Planetary Age builds the worlds. From about nine to eleven billion years after the Big Bang — in our corner of the Galaxy, starting 4.6 billion years ago — a spinning disk of gas and stardust around the young Sun condensed, collided, and settled into eight planets, a Moon, oceans, and sky. Four lessons and seven milestones trace that construction — and end by finding the same story repeated around thousands of other stars."
    client:load
  />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="home">The View from Home</h2>
      <p>The <strong>Planetary Age</strong> — Chapter 4 of <em>Our Cosmic History</em> — runs from roughly
        <strong>nine to eleven billion years</strong> after the Big Bang, and across <strong>four lessons</strong> and
        <strong>seven milestones</strong> it tells how the stardust forged in Chapter 3 gathered into worlds. It begins
        exactly where the Stellar Age ended (<a href={subPath("3.4")}>§3.4</a>): around 9 Gyr — about
        <strong>4.6 billion years ago</strong> — a chemically rich cloud in the Milky Way collapsed to ignite our Sun,
        leaving behind a spinning disk of gas and dust. Everything this chapter describes was built from that disk.</p>
      <p>So we start with the finished product: home. The solar system is one star's household — four rocky worlds and
        a belt of rubble close in, four giants and a belt of ice farther out, all riding a single flat disk that is
        itself the fossil of the nebula — wrapped in the vast bubble blown by the solar wind and, beyond everything,
        the spherical Oort cloud of comets, reaching a third of the way to the next star.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("4.1")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">4.1</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">The Solar System</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="families">Families of Worlds</h2>
      <p>Just as sorting the stars by two numbers revealed their families (<a href={subPath("3.2")}>§3.2</a>), sorting
        planets by size and orbit sorts the more than <strong>6,300 worlds</strong> now known into clean types: hot
        Jupiters roasting beside their stars, cold gas and ice giants like our own, the super-Earths our system
        strangely lacks, lava worlds, possible ocean worlds, and the rare Earth-likes. A third number —
        <strong>density</strong>, mass divided by volume — betrays what each is made of, from Saturn (lighter than
        water) to iron-hearted Earth. Two case studies from home close the lesson: Saturn, a miniature solar system of
        rings and shepherd moons, and Earth, whose churning core, layered air, and magnetic shield keep it alive.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("4.2")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">4.2</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">Types of Planets</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="assembly">Building Worlds</h2>
      <p>How did a disk of dust become all of that? In the <strong>accretion of planetesimals</strong> (9.1 Gyr),
        microscopic grains stuck together into pebbles, kilometre-sized bodies, and then protoplanets — rock alone
        inside the snow line, rock plus ice beyond it, which is why the giants grew first and biggest. Meanwhile the
        young Sun threw a tantrum: in its violent <strong>T-Tauri phase</strong> (9.1 Gyr) its fierce early wind swept
        the leftover gas from the disk — capping the giants' growth and stripping the first, flimsy atmospheres of the
        rocky worlds. The young Earth, melted by impacts and radioactivity, then sorted itself: in <strong>planetary
        differentiation</strong> (9.2 Gyr) the iron sank to build the core — the future magnetic dynamo — while
        lighter rock floated up as mantle and crust.</p>
      <p>Then came the blow that made the night sky: a Mars-sized protoplanet, <strong>Theia</strong>, slammed into
        the young Earth, and the splashed-out debris gathered into <strong>the Moon</strong> (9.2 Gyr) — the partner
        that has steadied Earth's tilt ever since. Volcanoes exhaled a new atmosphere, and as it cooled, planet-wide
        rains — topped up by icy comet deliveries — filled the basins to form the first <strong>oceans</strong>
        (9.4 Gyr). The finale was rough: the <strong>Late Heavy Bombardment</strong> (9.6–10 Gyr) battered every inner
        world with leftover asteroids and comets, until the barrage faded and Earth's crust thickened into stable
        continents — the <strong>stabilization of the lithosphere</strong> (10–11 Gyr), with the first supercontinent,
        Vaalbara — turning a hellish sphere into a quiet world ready for chemistry.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("4.3")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">4.3</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">Formation and Evolution of Planets</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="other-suns">Worlds of Other Suns</h2>
      <p>For most of history our eight planets were the only ones known. This chapter's telescope changed that:
        <strong>Kepler</strong>, launched in 2009, stared at about <strong>150,000 stars at once</strong> and watched
        for the tiny, repeating dips — as small as twenty parts in a million — of a planet crossing its star's face.
        By its end in 2018 it had confirmed nearly 2,800 planets and proved that planets outnumber stars in the
        Galaxy. Together with the wobble method that found the first exoplanet around a Sun-like star in 1995, and the
        coronagraph portraits that photograph young giants directly, it turned "are there other worlds?" into a
        catalogue — and handed the next age its question: on worlds of rock and water at comfortable distances from
        steady stars, what does chemistry do next?</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("4.4")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">4.4</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">Discovering Exoplanets</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <hr />

      <blockquote><p>The Stellar Age forged the elements; the Planetary Age gathered them into worlds — a disk
        condensed into eight planets, one battered young Earth grew a Moon, oceans, and continents, and the same
        construction, we now know, has played out around thousands of other suns.</p></blockquote>
    </div>
  </article>

